    description: "New England fieldstone foundations are repaired by stopping water intrusion, repointing deteriorated mortar with lime-based mixes, replacing failed stones, and underpinning where settlement has occurred.",
    h1: "How Are New England Fieldstone Foundations Repaired?",
    answer: "A New England fieldstone foundation is a wall of irregular stones laid in mortar - sometimes with little more than gravity and lime holding them together - supporting houses that have stood for 150 to 250 years. The stones themselves rarely fail; the mortar between them does, crumbling from water, frost, and age until stones loosen and the wall loses its integrity. Direct answer: the engineer first stops the water and soil movement causing the damage, then specifies repointing or rebuilding of deteriorated joints with lime-based mortar, replacement of collapsed or missing stones, interior drainage and waterproofing, and underpinning or supplemental footings where settlement has occurred - with the design accounting for frost depth and reviewed by the local building official.\n\nDiagnosis comes before repair, because the same cracked foundation can come from three different causes needing three different fixes. The engineer distinguishes settlement cracking (diagonal, wider at one end, often near a downspout or a filled-in well), frost heave (horizontal displacement near grade where the footing sits above frost depth), and simple mortar deterioration (joints that rake out by hand while the stones stay put). Bulging walls get measured for plumb over their height; a wall that has moved inches inward under soil pressure needs structural stabilization, not just repointing.\n\nRepointing is the core repair and the mortar choice matters enormously. Old fieldstone walls were laid in soft lime mortar that flexes and breathes with the stones; repointing with hard modern Portland cement traps moisture behind a rigid joint, and the trapped water freezes, expands, and pops the stone faces off. The engineer specifies a lime-based mortar matched to the original in strength and permeability, and requires joints to be raked to sound material before the new mortar goes in - pointing over dust is a repair that fails within a few seasons. Where stones have fallen out or whole sections have collapsed, the wall is rebuilt stone by stone, with the engineer verifying bearing and wall thickness as the rebuild progresses.\n\nWater management is half the repair. Exterior excavation and waterproofing is the thorough answer but often impractical against old stone, so the engineer commonly designs an interior perimeter drain tied to a sump, sealed wall coverings that channel seepage to the drain, and grading and gutter work outside to move roof water away from the foundation. Underpinning addresses settlement: new concrete sections extend the stone wall's bearing to deeper, stable soil in short sequenced segments. The authority having jurisdiction - usually the town or city building official - reviews structural foundation work, and in New England's many small towns the submittal path can be a single part-time building inspector rather than a big-city plan review office, with timelines to match.",
