        body: "Capacity upgrades bring the building's systems into the modern era. Electrical service is upsized for the calculated load - new panels, arc-fault and ground-fault protection to current code, and dedicated circuits for HVAC equipment, laundry, and kitchens that the original knob-and-tube or early Romex system never anticipated. The engineer performs formal load calculations rather than guessing, because an oversized service wastes money on utility upgrades while an undersized one leaves the building constrained.\n\nHeating and cooling equipment is selected for the envelope as it actually performs. Historic buildings with uninsulated masonry walls and original windows have heating loads dominated by envelope losses, so the engineer models the real assembly - not a code-minimum wall - and selects equipment accordingly, whether that is a high-efficiency boiler reusing radiators, air-source heat pumps sized for Rhode Island's design temperatures, or a hybrid. Controls get modernized in parallel: programmable thermostats or building controls, ventilation meeting current indoor air quality expectations, and domestic hot water designed for the fixture count. Commissioning at the end verifies each system performs as designed before the walls close.",
