        question: "Why do hillside homes and buildings slide in Pittsburgh?",
        answer: "The common triggers are water and added weight: saturated soils lose strength, so heavy rains or broken water lines can destabilize a marginally stable slope; unpermitted fill or new construction adds weight to the top of a slope; and excavations at the toe remove the soil buttressing the hillside. Pittsburgh's geology - clay-rich soils and ancient landslide deposits on steep slopes - makes many hillsides sensitive to these changes, which is why drainage and geotechnical review matter so much.",
