        heading: "Elevation, Wet Floodproofing, and Openings",
        body: "The vertical zoning of a flood-resilient Seaport building is deliberate: everything the owner cannot afford to lose sits above the design flood elevation, and everything below it is designed to flood and recover. Ground floors are built with flood-resistant materials — concrete, tile, and marine-grade finishes instead of drywall and carpet — and wall systems include flood vents that let water in and out so hydrostatic pressure cannot push walls off their foundations. Breakaway walls in enclosures below elevated buildings are designed to fail under flood loads without damaging the structure above, which the structural engineer verifies through the code's flood-load provisions.\n\nEvery opening below the design flood elevation is a designed element, not an afterthought. Pedestrian doors get deployable flood shields with gasketed frames anchored to the structure; garage and loading entrances get barrier systems sized for the full hydrostatic head; and plumbing systems get backwater valves on every lateral below the flood elevation so the municipal sewer cannot reverse into the building. Elevator pits are sealed and their equipment placed above the flood line, and emergency generators are elevated or protected, because life-safety systems must function during and after the event. The engineer documents each of these measures in the permit set so the reviewer can verify the complete flood strategy.",
