        body: "Construction phasing is a design constraint, not just a schedule. The engineer structures the work so the building remains occupiable: summer shutdown windows carry the heavy demolition and equipment replacement, while tie-ins to existing systems are scheduled for breaks with temporary heating or cooling bridging the gaps. Phased areas are isolated with temporary barriers and dust control, and the design sequences system startups so each phase is tested before the next begins. The bid documents define the phasing explicitly, because in public bidding the contractor prices exactly what the documents show — ambiguity becomes change orders.\n\nPennsylvania's public-bid and prevailing-wage requirements shape the entire delivery. The engineer's specifications must allow competitive bidding among qualified contractors, document prevailing-wage compliance, and structure alternates and allowances the way public owners evaluate them. Submittals, testing, and commissioning are written as enforceable contract requirements rather than suggestions. Permitting goes through Philadelphia's Department of Licenses and Inspections, where plan review covers the mechanical, electrical, plumbing, and energy-code compliance of the new systems; the authority having jurisdiction signs off at each inspection stage, and the engineer supports the contractor through review comments and field questions until final approval.",
