        heading: "Piers, Berthing, and the Working Waterfront",
        body: "Pier structural design starts with the working loads: forklifts with palletized seafood, ice deliveries, fuel trucks, and stacked gear, combined with berthing forces from vessels coming alongside and environmental forces from ice, waves, and storms. The engineer designs pile bents for vertical load and lateral berthing energy, sizes the deck for the heaviest vehicle plus impact, and details fendering — timber, rubber, or composite systems — to absorb vessel impact without transmitting damage into the piles. In salt water, durability detailing is structural design: the specification covers pile materials and treatments, corrosion protection for steel, and connection details that can be inspected and maintained, because a pier that cannot be inspected is a pier whose lifespan is a guess.\n\nBerthing geometry follows the fleet. The engineer works with the harbor and the operators to set berth lengths, water depths at mean low water, and fender spacing for the vessels that actually use the facility — from day boats to larger draggers — and designs the utilities each berth needs: potable water, power, and pump-out or waste reception. Shore power and lighting are designed for the marine environment, and the layout keeps working berths separate from any public access so commercial operations and visitors do not conflict. The buildings behind the pier — processing, cold storage, ice, gear repair — are sited so product flows downhill operationally from vessel to truck with minimum handling.",
