        body: "The boring program is the foundation of the foundation design. Deep borings along the waterfront typically reveal fill and soft river clays near the surface, transitioning with depth to denser sands and gravels and eventually to bedrock — and the pile design targets the competent layer that can reliably carry the building. The geotechnical engineer establishes the bearing stratum's elevation across the site, because it varies, and provides the soil parameters the structural engineer uses to calculate end-bearing and skin-friction capacity for each candidate pile type. Where the bearing layer is deep or variable, the design may specify piles driven to a defined resistance criterion rather than a fixed tip elevation, so every pile proves its own capacity during installation.\n\nThe structural design turns capacity into a buildable foundation. The engineer lays out the pile groups under each column, designs the reinforced-concrete pile caps that distribute column loads into the piles, and details the connections for uplift as well as compression — because flood buoyancy and wind overturning can pull on piles that are usually thought of as pushing down. Corrosion detailing reflects the environment: steel piles get protective coatings or increased sacrificial thickness in the splash and tidal zones, and concrete piles use durable mixes. The specifications define the installation criteria, the testing program, and the acceptance standards, so the piles the contractor installs are the piles the engineer designed.",
