        heading: "Water Service, Boosters, and Sewer Connections",
        body: "The domestic water design is a zoned pumping system. The engineer calculates the peak demand for each zone from fixture counts and occupancy, selects booster pump sets — typically duplex or triplex with standby capacity — and places intermediate storage tanks that buffer demand and provide the reserves fire protection requires. Each zone's piping is sized for velocity limits that prevent noise and water hammer, and pressure-reducing valves at zone entries and sometimes at individual floors keep pressures within the comfortable and code-compliant range. Hot water follows the same zoning, with heat exchangers or heaters per zone and recirculation loops balanced so the farthest fixture still gets hot water promptly.\n\nThe connection to the city is its own engineered scope. Water services enter through metered connections sized for the building's peak demand, with backflow-prevention assemblies — reduced-pressure-zone devices for the domestic service — protecting the public mains, all detailed for DEP approval and testing access. The sewer connection is designed for the building's total discharge with the DEP's requirements for the lateral, and in combined-sewer districts the design addresses the city's stormwater detention expectations so the tower does not surge the combined system during cloudbursts. The engineer sequences the DOB plumbing review and the DEP service approvals together, because the building cannot be commissioned or occupied until both authorities have signed off on their pieces.",
